# **The same invoice claimed twice** — Plan 123 Slice H.
#
# One invoice, two register rows, credit taken on both. No statutory section
# names this, because the statute assumes each supply is claimed once; it is a
# policy finding for that reason.
#
# It arises without anyone intending it — the same purchase entered under two
# voucher numbers, a document re-imported after a correction, a branch and
# head office both booking a shared invoice. That is exactly what makes it
# worth a rule: nobody is looking for it.
#
# **Keyed on (supplier, invoice number), not on the subject IRI.** Two rows
# that produced the *same* subject are one fact asserted twice and are
# invisible here by construction — which is correct, because the graph has
# already merged them. What this finds is two *distinct* subjects claiming one
# invoice, which is the case a store keyed by subject can never surface.
#
# `?a != ?b` alone would report each pair twice, once in each order. `STR(?a) <
# STR(?b)` picks one ordering and reports the pair once — the same reason
# every self-join in this pack that could double-count carries one.

PREFIX gst: <https://graph-owl.dev/packs/gst#>

SELECT ?invoice ?number ?gstin ?taxAmount ?duplicateOf
WHERE {
  GRAPH ?register {
    ?invoice a gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
             gst:issuedBy      ?supplier ;
             gst:invoiceNumber ?number ;
             gst:taxAmount     ?taxAmount .
    ?supplier gst:supplierGstin ?gstin .

    ?duplicateOf a gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
                 gst:issuedBy      ?supplier ;
                 gst:invoiceNumber ?number .
  }

  FILTER (STR(?invoice) < STR(?duplicateOf))
}
ORDER BY ?number
